Overlay patch 0032 - the two letterboxes overlay 0029 could not reach.

CLASS: (b) upstream bug fix worth sending, same as 0029 and for the same reason:
it is a rendering correctness fix, and `__IOS__`-gating it would put the macOS
oracle (the spec's ground truth) permanently out of parity with the phone.

WHAT THE USER STILL SAW ON 0.0.0.9
--------------------------------
Overlay 0029 removed the letterbox from the CAMERA (the viewport and the
scissor). Two other places paint the 4:3 frame by hand, and neither goes through
the camera, so both survived:

(a) **Outdoor maps still band top and bottom.** `gfx_draw_background`
    (`src/background_gfx.c`) clamps the backdrop's `backgroundMinY` /
    `backgroundMaxY` to `camera->viewportStartY .. + viewportH` - the FIELDS,
    which 0029 deliberately leaves at the map's 20..220 - and then EXPLICITLY
    fills black above and below them:

        // Black letterbox above/below the play area, full visible width.
        if (backgroundMinY > 0)               fill(wsLeft, 0, wsRight-1, backgroundMinY-1);
        if (backgroundMaxY < SCREEN_HEIGHT)   fill(wsLeft, backgroundMaxY, wsRight-1, SCREEN_HEIGHT-1);

    Reached by every map that ships no background header (`world.c:215` calls
    `set_background_size(..., SCREEN_INSET_X, SCREEN_INSET_Y)`), by every battle
    (`state_battle.c:191`, same call) and by the pause screen
    (`state_pause.c:210`), plus any map whose background header starts at row 20.
    The X axis of the very same block was already widened - `get_cam_frame_x`
    returns the true screen edges once the view is widened - so this is the
    identical half-finished widescreen fit 0029 found in the camera, one file
    over. See MEASURED below: this half is reasoned, not reproduced.

(b) **The map-transition wipe leaves a strip of the old map top and bottom.**
    `appendGfx_screen_overlay`'s `OVERLAY_VIEWPORT_COLOR` case
    (`src/screen_overlays.c`) fills from `camera->viewportStartY` to
    `+ camera->viewportH`, i.e. rows 20..220 of 240, while its X extents are
    `OTRGetRectDimensionFrom{Left,Right}Edge(0)` - the true widened screen
    edges. So the fade covers the full width and only the 4:3 height, and 20
    rows of the outgoing map stay lit at the top and the bottom: 20/240 of a
    1260 px drawable is ~105 px, which is the strip the user measured at ~75.
    `OVERLAY_SCREEN_COLOR` immediately above it already fills
    `0 .. SCREEN_HEIGHT - 1`, which is what the viewport case should do when the
    viewport IS the screen.

THE FIX
-------
One predicate, in `cam_main.c` beside `get_cam_frame_x`, exported like it:

    b32 cam_frame_fills_height(s32 camID);

- true exactly when 0029's own two hunks fire: the view is widened past 4:3,
  the camera reaches the screen edges, and its viewport is the standard
  full-size world/battle one (`viewportH >= SCREEN_HEIGHT - 2*SCREEN_INSET_Y`).
  It is the Y counterpart of `get_cam_frame_x`, and it is written in terms of
  the same two static predicates 0029 uses, so the three call sites cannot drift
  apart.

Then (a) lets the backdrop span the full height (which removes both black fills
by making their conditions false, rather than by deleting them), and (b) fills
the whole screen.

WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO
--------------------------------
* No camera field is written. `viewportStartY` / `viewportH` still say what the
  map said, exactly as after 0029.
* The game's own letterboxes are untouched: the demo camera's 177-tall
  viewport, the title and file select's 184, the intro's 162, and every
  `cam_api` viewport a cutscene sets all fail the `viewportH` test, so they
  still frame themselves. 4:3 and narrower fails `cam_view_is_widened()` and
  keeps Paper Mario's frame on all four sides.
* `OVERLAY_SCREEN_COLOR` and the stencil transitions are not touched - they
  already use the full screen.

MEASURED, ON THE SIMULATOR (lane 2, iPhone Air iOS 27.0)
--------------------------------------------------------
The wipe is the case that could be A/B'd, and it is unambiguous. Same fresh
container, same new game, same automated drive; the patch FILE moved out of
`overlay/patches/` for the "before" build, because `build-sim.sh` re-applies the
overlay (D27's methodology note).

BEFORE (`artifacts/sim/m028-g-wipe-BEFORE-105px-strips.png`, the standard
map-change fade at Mario's house): row-mean brightness over the central columns
is ~160 for rows 0-105, ~80 for rows 110-1155, ~190 for rows 1160-1259. I.e.
**a 105 px strip of the outgoing map at the top and a ~100 px strip at the
bottom are not covered at all**, and 105/1260 = 20/240 = SCREEN_INSET_Y - the
same constant as D27.

AFTER (`artifacts/sim/m028-h-wipe-AFTER-full-height.png`): the same fade covers
the frame top to bottom - the median column is dark for all 1260 rows, and the
9.4 % of the image that is not dark is the UIKit touch overlay, which is not part
of the game frame at all.

No regression anywhere else: 71 driven gameplay frames across the prologue
(Mario's house yard, its interior, the castle party) measure 0 px top and bottom
with the patch in (`m028-i-yard-no-bands.png` is the yard), the title and file
select still letterbox, and the attract demo still plays on its own stage.

**The outdoor-band case (a) was NOT reproduced on the simulator.** Mario's house
yard - the map the user's screenshot shows - takes a background path that does not
hit `gfx_draw_background`'s letterbox fills, and 240 automated frames over two
builds never produced a banded outdoor frame. The fills are nonetheless the only
remaining hand-drawn 4:3 frame in the world renderer, they are reached by every
map that ships no background header (`world.c:215`), by every battle
(`state_battle.c:191`) and by the pause screen (`state_pause.c:210`), and they
are the exact same half-finished widescreen fit as the camera's. The hunk is in
because the code is certainly wrong; which map the user was on is a question for
his next device run, not a reason to leave it wrong.

WHERE THE HUNKS SIT
-------------------
Three files.
* `src/cam_main.c`: one hunk immediately after `get_cam_frame_x` (~:115
  pristine). Overlay 0029's two hunks are at ~:163 and ~:229 - clear.
* `src/background_gfx.c` and `src/screen_overlays.c`: no other patch in the
  series touches either file.
* `include/functions.h`: the declaration, beside `get_cam_frame_x` (~:455). No
  other patch touches this header.

